gdi32: D3DKMTSetProcessSchedulingPriorityClass exists and succeeds.

Steam's client service (bin/SteamService.dll, 32-bit, the client of
2026-10-31 in a Highball environment) imports
GDI32.D3DKMTSetProcessSchedulingPriorityClass by name. Neither CrossOver 26.3
nor upstream Wine master (gdi32.spec, 2026-11-12) exports it, so Wine's
loader binds the import to a stub that ends the process when called: "wine:
Call from 7BE7E998 to unimplemented function
GDI32.dll.D3DKMTSetProcessSchedulingPriorityClass, aborting". In highball#699
that line came while Satisfactory's Steam overlay was opening on the Wine 11
engine r31 (M5, macOS 27.2), followed by Steam's "CSteamEngine::BMainLoop
appears to have stalled > 15 seconds", and the game froze. That the abort
caused the freeze is a reading of the log, not measured.

The service already handles a failure ("SteamService:
D3DKMTSetProcessSchedulingPriorityClass failed with status=%d!"), so a stub
that answers is enough; it answers success, as Windows does for a caller that
may set the class. Measured before the patch: GetProcAddress for the name
returns NULL on the Wine 11 engine r31 and the Wine 10 engine r28.

Upstream: not sent.

